<node CREATED="1791916967975" ID="ID_1317211255" MODIFIED="1791916971472" TEXT="Day Planner"/>
<node CREATED="1791917047315" ID="ID_979074385" MODIFIED="1791917047315" TEXT="Standard Index and Divider Tabs">
<node CREATED="1791917047315" ID="ID_532672584" MODIFIED="1791917047315" TEXT="&#x2022; A&#x2013;Z Index Dividers: Usually available in cream, white, or marbled card stock with 13-tab (2 letters per tab) or full alphabetical layouts."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047315" ID="ID_1441241774" MODIFIED="1791917047315" TEXT="&#x2022; Numerical / Subject Dividers: Numbered tabs (1&#x2013;6 or 1&#x2013;12) or generic colored/marbled index tabs."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047315" ID="ID_1099274379" MODIFIED="1791917047315" TEXT="&#x2022; Blank Tab Dividers: Customisable card or plastic tabs where owners could write or label their own categories."/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1653191894" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="Common Planner and Organisation Sections">
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1142770160" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Diary &amp; Calendar: Week-to-view, day-per-page, month-to-view, and year planners."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1209665118" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Name, Address &amp; Telephone: Contact pages with alphabetical tabs."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1255497712" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Notes &amp; Paper: Plain, ruled (lined), quadrille (graph), and tinted/pastel notepaper."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1131095381" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; To-Do / Action Lists: Priority task lists, project action sheets, and meeting planners."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_79287075" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Financial &amp; Expense Tracking: Personal expense sheets, budget planners, and account record pages."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1938635720" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Travel &amp; Maps: London Underground (Tube) and intercity rail maps, UK/European road maps, and world/political time-zone maps."/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791917047316" ID="ID_1090809393" MODIFIED="1791917047316" TEXT="&#x2022; Specialised / Niche Inserts: Medical/lab records, wine lists, restaurant guides, project management sheets, and address/birthday reminders."/>
<node CREATED="1791917047317" MODIFIED="1791917047317" TEXT="&#x2022; Special Lineups: Children&apos;s and youth spin-off systems from the early &apos;90s like the Funfax (interchangeable subject books) and Spy File (secret diary, agency notes, and code names)."/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791917964256" ID="ID_372804026" MODIFIED="1791918017262" TEXT="Communication">
<node CREATED="1791917989088" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_364369016" MODIFIED="1791921632432" TEXT="PayPhone">
<node CREATED="1791921513032" ID="ID_1189094555" MODIFIED="1791921514734" TEXT=" The Public Payphone Math (1980s&#x2013;1990s)  To complete the stealth communication loop, a player using a pager requires access to a public payphone. In the 1980s and 1990s, payphones were ubiquitous infrastructure across the UK, operated primarily by British Telecom (BT) and later joined by Mercury Communications.  &#xd83e;&#xde99; 1980s: Cash vs. Early Phonecards  &#x2022; The Cost: In the mid-1980s, the minimum call charge at a BT telephone box was 10p. &#x2022; The Mechanics (Cash): The player had to physically carry heavy loose change (10p and 50p coins). &#x2022; The Innovation (BT Phonecards): Introduced in 1981, these used a green optical strip that the phone box physically &quot;melted&quot; or erased as credits were used. A standard 20-unit card cost &#xa3;2.00, and a 100-unit card cost &#xa3;10.00 (~8.5% of a monthly benefit). &#x2022; Tracking Profile: Zero Trackability. Using a cash-operated payphone or a standard, cash-purchased Phonecard left an entirely anonymous data trail. The police could only log that someone called a number from an exact street corner phone box at a specific time.  &#xd83d;&#xdcb3; 1990s: Smart Cards &amp; Phreaking  &#x2022; The Cost: The minimum call charge rose to 20p in the 1990s. &#x2022; The Mechanics: In 1989/1990, BT switched to microchip-based &quot;Smart&quot; Phonecards. &#x2022; The Exploit (&quot;Phreaking&quot;): In game terms, this introduces an Illegal Exploitation Tier. In the 1990s, hackers figured out how to clone BT Phonecards, apply magnetic tape or nail varnish tricks, or use custom microchips to get free calls. &#x2022; Tracking Profile: Low-to-Medium Trackability. If a player repeatedly used a compromised/hacked card or a specific phone box for illegal operations, police could flag the card ID or set up physical stakes at that specific kiosk.  &#xd83e;&#xddee; &quot;Surveillance Heat&quot; Game Engine Formula  To calculate how much &quot;Heat&quot; (suspicion/police visibility) a player accumulates based on their tech choices, you can use a basic dynamic formula. Every time a player performs an action (makes a call, receives a page, triggers an illegal event), it generates Base Heat (\(H_{B}\)). The final Operational Heat (\(H_{O}\)) is mitigated or multiplied by their hardware setup and location stability. \(\text{Operational\ Heat\ }(H_{O})=(H_{B}\times M_{\text{Device}})-R_{\text{Mobility}}\)  1. Device Multipliers (\(M_{\text{Device}}\))  The type of hardware used dictates how easily the network logs the identity.  Device Type&#x9;1980s Multiplier&#x9;1990s Multiplier&#x9;2000s Multiplier&#x9;Economic Context Contract Mobile&#x9;2.0 (Instant Link)&#x9;1.5 (Fast Data Access)&#x9;2.0 (Advanced Dragnets)&#x9;High cost, tied to real ID. PAYG / Burner Mobile&#x9;N/A&#x9;0.5 (Unregistered)&#x9;1.2 (Tower Dumps/IMSI)&#x9;Medium cost, anonymous in &apos;90s. Pager (Text/Numeric)&#x9;0.1 (Passive Only)&#x9;0.0 (Untraceable)&#x9;0.5 (Obsolete/High Profile)&#x9;Low/No upkeep. Payphone (Cash/Card)&#x9;0.0 (Anonymous)&#x9;0.0 (Anonymous)&#x9;0.5 (Cameras near kiosks)&#x9;Micro-transaction per call.   2. Mobility Reduction (\(R_{\text{Mobility}}\))  If a player stays in the same place, cell towers log repeated connections, increasing predictability. If they move, they shake off location triangulation. &#x2022; Static (R = 0): Using a phone from a safehouse or staying in the same map node for multiple turns. &#x2022; Mobile (R = 10 to 30): Moving to a different map quadrant or cell zone between calls.  &#xd83c;&#xdfae; The Optimal 1990s Stealth &quot;Game Loop&quot;  By combining these systems, you create a deeply satisfying, multi-step mechanical sequence for players looking to manage their resources and dodge law enforcement: [Character receives an Illegal Mission Request]        &#x2502;        &#x25bc; [Pager emits a silent text alert: &quot;MEET AT THE DOCKS 10PM&quot;] &#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x25ba; (Generates 0 Heat)        &#x2502;        &#x25bc; [Player must physically travel to a Public Payphone node] &#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x25ba; (Requires 20p or Phonecard Item)        &#x2502;        &#x25bc; [Player dials out to confirm the job acceptance] &#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x25ba; (Generates 0 Heat, but locks character in place) If the player tries to shortcut this by simply using a registered 1980s/1990s Contract Mobile Phone from their house, they save time and travel actions, but their Operational Heat (\(H_{O}\)) skyrockets, accelerating an inevitable police raid on their safehouse. Would you like me to map out the inventory items required for these eras (such as different types of Phonecards, spare change, or replacement SIM packs) and their relative costs in your game&apos;s economy? "/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791917995776" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_941006798" MODIFIED="1791921636520" TEXT="Basic Mobile Phone">
<node CREATED="1791918706224" ID="ID_792774672" MODIFIED="1791918748558" TEXT=" 1980s: The Analog Voice Era (1G)  Phones in this decade were status symbols&#x2014;large, heavy &quot;bricks&quot; primarily installed in luxury cars or carried by corporate executives. &#x2022; Analogue Voice Calls: The absolute core function. Calling was expensive and plagued by poor audio quality and frequent dropouts. &#x2022; Basic Number Memory: Mainstream models could save a very limited directory of phone numbers (usually between 30 and 100) directly to the internal hardware memory. &#x2022; Physical Keypad &amp; Small Digital Screen: Backlit physical buttons coupled with a tiny, segment-based LED or LCD screen that showed dialed numbers and battery life. "/>
<node CREATED="1791918754216" ID="ID_1303628245" MODIFIED="1791918765168" TEXT="1990s: The Digital Text &amp; Customisation Era (2G)  The shift to digital GSM networks made phones smaller, affordable, and massively mainstream. &#x2022; SMS Text Messaging: First introduced in 1992, short text messaging (up to 160 characters) quickly became the defining feature of youth culture. &#x2022; Pre-installed Mobile Games: Phones introduced built-in, offline time-killers like the legendary Snake, Space Impact, and Tetris. &#x2022; Custom Ringtone Builders: Monophonic ringtones became popular, and users could manually compose melodies using Keypad code sheets. &#x2022; Basic Productivity Utilities: Standard additions included simple digital alarm clocks, basic mathematical calculators, and stopwatches. &#x2022; Caller ID &amp; Voicemail: Digital networks allowed users to see who was calling before answering and leave audio recordings when unavailable. "/>
<node CREATED="1791918767929" ID="ID_1455693454" MODIFIED="1791918781084" TEXT=" 2000s: The Multimedia &quot;Feature Phone&quot; Era (2G to 3G)  During this decade, phones swallowed peripheral devices, resulting in highly experimental phone shapes (sliders, flips, and swivels). &#x2022; Integrated Digital Cameras: Spurred on by the introduction of the first camera phones, taking low-resolution snapshots and sending them via MMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) became standard practice. &#x2022; MP3 Audio &amp; Media Playback: Phones replaced dedicated MP3 players. Users could store music tracks on early memory cards and set standard audio tracks as &quot;polyphonic&quot; or MP3 ringtones. &#x2022; Wireless Data &amp; Bluetooth: Bluetooth allowed local, wireless file-swapping and headset connections. &#x2022; Early &quot;WAP&quot; Web Browsing: Highly restricted, slow, text-heavy mobile web browsing over GPRS/EDGE networks became a staple button on the main keypad."/>
<node CREATED="1791918784672" ID="ID_1230227497" MODIFIED="1791918795759" TEXT="  2010s: The App &amp; Touchscreen Revolution (4G)  The landscape flattened into large glass slates dominated by capacitive touchscreens and continuous high-speed internet data ecosystems. &#x2022; App Stores &amp; Ecosystems: The phone became an open platform. Everyday utility was defined by user-downloaded applications for social media, mapping, and productivity. &#x2022; GPS &amp; Turn-by-Turn Navigation: Built-in GPS sensors made mapping apps standard, effectively killing the dedicated dashboard sat-nav industry. &#x2022; HD Multi-Lens Photography &amp; Video Streaming: High-definition video recording, front-facing &quot;selfie&quot; cameras, and mobile HD video streaming over 4G became universal. &#x2022; Contactless Mobile Payments: NFC chips allowed the phone to function safely as a digital wallet via services like Apple Pay and Google Wallet. &#x2022; Biometric Security: Fast unlocking via capacitive fingerprint scanners and early facial recognition replaced standard security PINs"/>
<node CREATED="1791918799585" ID="ID_992986109" MODIFIED="1791918811063" TEXT=".  2020s: The AI, Ecosystem &amp; Continuity Era (5G)  Modern standard phones are defined by invisible automation, ambient computing, and massive bandwidth. &#x2022; On-Device Generative AI: Native system integration of AI models that handle live conversational assistance, real-time voice translation, and smart photo editing (such as object removal). &#x2022; Computational Photography: Phone software takes multiple photos simultaneously, instantly processing them to balance low-light settings and night environments. &#x2022; Health &amp; Biosensing Integration: Advanced step tracking, sleep tracking, crash detection, and emergency satellite SOS messaging operate seamlessly in the background. &#x2022; Cross-Device Continuity: Seamless copying and pasting between a laptop and phone, wireless casting, and smart-home command hubs are standard, out-of-the-box system features. "/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791918020672" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_957571815" MODIFIED="1791922234115" TEXT="Pager">
<node CREATED="1791921352056" ID="ID_970761576" MODIFIED="1791921374208" TEXT="Integrating pagers into your game&apos;s economy provides an excellent middle-tier communication layer. In the real world, pagers acted as a vital economic stepping stone. They allowed low-income users to remain contactable for jobs or social life before mobile phones became truly affordable. Pagers used two distinct economic setups: 1. Subscription Model (1980s&#x2013;Early 1990s): You bought the hardware and paid a monthly line rental. 2. &quot;Free-to-Receive&quot; Pay-As-You-Go Model (Mid 1990s&#x2013;2000s): You bought the hardware upfront with no monthly fees. Instead, the person sending you the message paid a premium rate call charge (e.g., 50p per minute) to transmit the beep or text. This was an economic game-changer for people on benefits, transferring the ongoing operational cost to the caller.  &#xd83d;&#xdea8; 1980s: The Tone-Only / Numeric &quot;Beep&quot; Era  In this era, pagers had no screen or a tiny numeric-only display. When it beeped, you received a series of tones (meaning &quot;Call the office&quot;) or a phone number. You then had to find a physical public phone box to call back. &#x2022; Monthly Benefit: ~&#xa3;115 &#x2022; Hardware Cost (Upfront): ~&#xa3;100 to &#xa3;150 (85% to 130% of monthly benefit). &#x2022; Monthly Subscription: ~&#xa3;10 to &#xa3;15 per month (8.5% to 13% of monthly benefit). &#x2022; Economic Profile: Primarily a luxury or B2B business tool (doctors, IT support, drug dealers). Too expensive as a passive drain for a low-income build.  &#xd83d;&#xdcdf; Mid-1990s: The Alphanumeric &quot;Text Pager&quot; Boom  This is the golden era for your game&#x2019;s balancing. Devices like the Vodafone Zap or BT EasyReach allowed operators or computers to send full text sentences to a small LCD screen. The &quot;Free-to-Receive&quot; model removed monthly contract fees entirely. &#x2022; Monthly Benefit: ~&#xa3;195 &#x2022; Hardware Cost (Upfront): ~&#xa3;40 to &#xa3;80 single payment (20% to 40% of monthly benefit). &#x2022; Monthly Subscription: &#xa3;0 (Free to receive, callers pay premium phone rates to page you). &#x2022; Economic Profile: High utility / Low upkeep. This was the ultimate low-income strategy. A player saves a lump sum once, faces 0% monthly cash-flow drain, and permanently unlocks the ability to receive job offers or emergency alerts while away from home.  &#xd83d;&#xdcc9; 2000s: The Mobile Takeover &amp; Obsolescence  Mobile phone network bundles and cheap Pay-As-You-Go SIM cards cannibalised the consumer pager market. Pagers rapidly devolved into a niche utility. &#x2022; Monthly Benefit: ~&#xa3;260 &#x2022; Hardware Cost (Upfront): Dropped to clearance prices, ~&#xa3;15 to &#xa3;30 (5% to 11% of monthly benefit). &#x2022; Monthly Subscription: Still &#xa3;0 for legacy consumer systems, or ~&#xa3;5 for basic business numeric tiers. &#x2022; Economic Profile: Outclassed. While the monthly cost was lower than a &#xa3;25 mobile phone contract, the inability to reply without finding a landline meant choosing a pager actively reduced a character&apos;s efficiency compared to the rest of society.  &#xd83d;&#xdcca; Economic Model Comparison Summary   Era / Technology&#x9;Upfront Cost (% of Benefit)&#x9;Monthly Upkeep (% of Benefit)&#x9;Gameplay Mechanics &amp; Function 1980s Tone Pager&#x9;100% (Very High)&#x9;10% (Medium Drain)&#x9;Passive Alert Only: Must spend in-game cash at a phone box to respond. 1990s Text Pager&#x9;30% (Medium)&#x9;0% (None)&#x9;Asymmetric Communication: High efficiency for job hunters; text displays on screen. 2000s Pager&#x9;8% (Low)&#x9;0% to 2% (Negligible)&#x9;Obsolete Tier: Penalty to social/work speed compared to characters with mobiles.   &#xd83c;&#xdfae; How to use this in your Game Loop  If you want to code a progression ladder for a character starting with low resources, the optimal historical upgrade path looks like this: 1. Phase 1 (No Tech): Misses passive event triggers (missed job offers, slow relationship building). 2. Phase 2 (The 1990s Pager Hack): Player buys a Text Pager upfront. Upkeep is 0%. They can now catch all passive job offers instantly, but must travel to a landline to accept them. 3. Phase 3 (The Mobile Upgrade): Player transitions to a full mobile network plan, accepting a permanent ~10% monthly cash-flow tax in exchange for the power to action events anywhere instantly. Would you like me to map out how public payphones (and phonecard costs) fit into this 1980s/1990s math to complete the pager ecosystem? "/>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791921389775" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_1310968674" MODIFIED="1791921643559" TEXT="Trackability/risk">
<node CREATED="1791921401129" ID="ID_785461499" MODIFIED="1791921424863" TEXT="For your game mechanics, tracking and identity verification should serve as a major risk/reward system for players. Historically, there was a stark division between location tracking (where the device is physically located) and identity tracking (who actually owns or is using the device). Here is the operational breakdown of law enforcement capabilities by era, structured for easy integration into an economic or simulation game loop.  &#xd83d;&#xdce1; The 1980s: Easy Identity, Blind Location  In the 1980s, mobile networks were analog (1G) and highly centralized. Using a phone required a rigorous financial credit check, making identity tracking instant, while real-time location tracking was practically non-existent. &#x2022; Identifying the User (95% Success Rate): Because phones cost thousands of pounds and required steep monthly contracts, every phone was tied to a verified name, billing address, and bank account. If a criminal used their own phone, the police could get the subscriber log from the telecom provider immediately. &#x2022; Locating the Device (5% Success Rate): Early analog cell towers covered massive geographic zones (sometimes up to a 25-mile radius). The network only knew which massive macro-tower the phone was connected to. &#x2022; The Flaw (Eavesdropping Risk): Analog signals were completely unencrypted. Anyone with a cheap, commercially available radio scanner from an electronics shop could tune into a mobile frequency and listen to private voice calls. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Suggestion: High Identity Risk / Zero Location Risk. If a player uses a registered phone for illegal activities, their &quot;Wanted Level&quot; or &quot;Suspicion Meter&quot; spikes instantly. However, the police cannot physically ambush them unless they catch them static in one place for hours.  &#xd83d;&#xdcdf; The 1990s: The &quot;Ghost&quot; Pager Era  The 1990s was the ultimate era of asymmetric anonymity, driven by the introduction of digital GSM networks, Pay-As-You-Go SIM cards, and pagers. &#x2022; Identifying the User (0% to 10% Success Rate): This was the golden era of untraceable communication. Both text pagers and early PAYG mobile phones could be bought with cash at high-street shops with zero registration, no ID required, and no name attached. These became the original &quot;burner&quot; devices. &#x2022; Locating a Mobile Phone (40% Success Rate): Networks grew denser, introducing early Cell Tower Triangulation. By measuring signal strength across three adjacent towers, the police could narrow a phone&apos;s location down to a few city blocks in urban areas, though rural tracking remained entirely inaccurate. &#x2022; Locating a Pager (0% Success Rate): Pagers are entirely passive radio receivers. They do not transmit a signal back to a tower; they simply listen to broadcast waves. A pager emits no active signal to track. The police could intercept the message data floating through the airwaves, but they had absolutely no way to locate the physical pager unit itself. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Suggestion: The &quot;Ghost&quot; Build. Buying a Pager or a 1990s PAYG mobile with cash grants the player 100% identity anonymity. Pagers offer a perfect stealth mechanic: 0% chance of location tracking, but the player suffers a time-delay penalty because they must find a public payphone to reply.  &#xd83c;&#xdf9b;&#xfe0f; The 2000s: The Dawn of Network Dragnets  In the 2000s, law enforcement caught up with consumer technology. The UK passed laws allowing rapid, warrantless data access for intelligence services, and network infrastructure became incredibly precise. &#x2022; Identifying the User (60% Success Rate): While unregistered SIM cards still existed, police began utilizing &quot;Tower Dumps&quot;. If a crime occurred, police could pull the data log of every single SIM card connected to that local tower. By cross-referencing which &quot;anonymous&quot; SIMs frequently moved together or called known contacts, algorithms could deanonymize a user via data profiling. &#x2022; Locating the Device (80% Success Rate): Micro-cell towers were deployed across cities to handle massive mobile traffic. Triangulation became highly accurate&#x2014;narrowing a target&apos;s location down to a specific street corner or a couple of buildings. Additionally, police deployed early IMSI Catchers (Stingrays). These are mobile, suitcase-sized devices hidden in police vans that mimic cell towers, forcing all nearby phones to connect to them to instantly map a suspect&apos;s precise room or hiding spot. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Suggestion: High Passive Risk. Unregistered burner phones still protect a player&apos;s identity initially, but if they stay in one territory for too long, police IMSI-catcher vans will actively hunt their signal, forcing the player to constantly discard and replace their hardware.  &#xd83d;&#xdee0;&#xfe0f; Summary Matrix for Your Game Engine   Era&#x9;Device Type&#x9;Identity Risk (Who are you?)&#x9;Location Risk (Where are you?)&#x9;Police Surveillance Method 1980s&#x9;Analog Mobile&#x9;Extremely High (Contract logs)&#x9;None (Macro-tower zones)&#x9;Wiretapping / Radio Scanners 1980s&#x9;Tone Pager&#x9;High (Corporate subscription)&#x9;Zero (Passive receiver)&#x9;Intercepting operator lines 1990s&#x9;Digital Mobile (PAYG)&#x9;Low (Cash purchases)&#x9;Medium (Block triangulation)&#x9;Cell tower triangulation logs 1990s&#x9;Text Pager&#x9;Zero (No signup required)&#x9;Zero (Passive receiver)&#x9;Airwave signal grabbing 2000s&#x9;Feature/Smartphone&#x9;Medium (Data profiling)&#x9;High (Street-level accuracy)&#x9;IMSI Catchers / Tower Dumps  Would it be helpful to design a &quot;Police Heat&quot; math formula based on these eras, factoring in how moving between cells or switching devices lowers a player&apos;s visibility? AI responses may include mistakes. Learn more ."/>
</node>
</node>
<node CREATED="1791918124232" ID="ID_581170038" MODIFIED="1791918137010" TEXT="Old Radio Alarm Clock"/>
<node CREATED="1791921653856" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_348363988" MODIFIED="1791921837831" TEXT="The Fence">
<node CREATED="1791921664008" ID="ID_1409518501" MODIFIED="1791921680887" TEXT="In criminology and economic modeling, fences (receivers of stolen goods) are the true engine of property crime. Without a middleman to convert physical items into liquid cash, a thief&apos;s inventory is useless. Historically, fences evolved from local neighborhood shopkeepers into highly sophisticated, multinational logistical operations. For your game engine, you can structure fences across three distinct historical eras, each using a different business model, laundering method, and risk profile.  1. The 1980s: The &quot;Brick &amp; Mortar&quot; Front  In the 1980s, laundering stolen property required a physical storefront. Fences integrated stolen inventory directly into legitimate cash-heavy businesses. &#x2022; Primary Fronts: Independent pawnshops, secondhand jewelry stores, scrap metal yards, and market stalls. &#x2022; The Operation: A thief brought in stolen consumer electronics (VCRs, TVs) or jewelry. The fence paid a brutal 10% to 20% of the actual retail value in cash. The fence then mixed these items onto their shelves alongside legitimate stock, falsifying paper ledgers to show a fake &quot;cash purchase from an anonymous walk-in.&quot; &#x2022; The High-End Liquidation (Gold Smelting): For high-value gold and silver heist loot, specialized receivers used backroom crucibles to melt down distinct jewelry into generic bars. Once melted, the origin was erased, allowing it to be sold directly to legitimate bullion dealers. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Concept: High Overhead / Fixed Location. Players must travel to a specific physical node on the map to sell loot. The fence has a capped amount of cash per day and a high risk of police &quot;sting&quot; operations if a thief is followed to the shop.  2. The 1990s: The &quot;Car Boot &amp; Pub&quot; Network  The 1990s in the UK saw an explosion of decentralized, informal markets. The rise of car boot sales and casual urban trading networks allowed fences to move massive volumes without owning a permanent shop. &#x2022; Primary Fronts: Weekly car boot sales, pub backrooms, market wholesale distributors, and residential &quot;safe houses.&quot; &#x2022; The Operation: Fences acted as distributors. They would buy a truckload of stolen consumer goods (designer clothing, perfume, car stereos, or text pagers) from a gang of thieves. Instead of retailing it themselves, they sold bulk lots to a network of micro-fences who dispersed to different weekend car boot sales across the country. &#x2022; No Questions Asked Consumerism: This era normalized the consumer mindset of buying cheap goods out of the back of a van with &quot;no questions asked&quot;. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Concept: Decentralised / Time-Gated. Fences are only available on specific game ticks (e.g., &quot;Sunday Morning Markets&quot;). They offer better payout rates (25% to 35% of retail value) because their operational overhead is low, but they will only buy goods in specific bulk categories.  3. The 2000s to Now: The &quot;E-Commerce &amp; Digital Clearer&quot;  The internet completely revolutionized fencing. It eliminated the geographic limitations of selling stolen goods locally and stripped away the need for physical criminal networks. &#x2022; Primary Fronts: Online auction sites (early eBay), classified forums (Craigslist/Gumtree), social media marketplaces, and dedicated e-waste/refurbishment fronts. &#x2022; The Operation: Modern fences operate &quot;clean&quot; digital storefronts. When a thief steals smartphones or laptops, the fence uses software to factory-reset the devices, flash the firmware, and list them globally online within hours. Stolen luxury goods are shipped across borders to international buyers who have no idea the item is hot. &#x2022; The Commercial Fence: A massive contemporary sub-type is the e-commerce cargo thief pipeline. Fences buy entire stolen pallets of everyday goods (baby formula, cosmetics, power tools) from highway truck hijackings, create a fake wholesale company LLC, and sell them back onto online storefronts as legitimate overstock. &#x2022; &#xd83c;&#xdfae; Game Mechanic Concept: Instant Liquidation / High Digital Footprint. Players can sell stolen items instantly from their safehouse laptop for 40% to 50% of retail value. However, doing so generates a steady trickle of &quot;Digital Heat&quot; as algorithms track electronic serial numbers, requiring the player to periodically buy fake merchant IDs to avoid detection.  &#xd83d;&#xdcca; Fence Balancing Matrix for Your Game Design   Era&#x9;Payout Rate (% of Item Value)&#x9;Liquidation Speed&#x9;Primary Risk&#x9;Best Cargo Types 1980s&#x9;10% &#x2013; 20% (Low)&#x9;Instant (Cash hand-off)&#x9;Physical surveillance / Shop raids&#x9;Gold, Silver, VCRs, Jewelry 1990s&#x9;25% &#x2013; 35% (Medium)&#x9;Delayed (Must wait for market day)&#x9;Informants / Local police sweeps&#x9;Designer clothes, Tools, Car stereos 2000s+&#x9;40% &#x2013; 60% (High)&#x9;Variable (Escrow/Shipping time)&#x9;Cyber-forensics / Serial number tracking&#x9;Smartphones, Laptops, Palletized freight  Would you like to design a &quot;Fence Trust&quot; progression mechanic, where a player unlocking better relationship tiers gets lower laundering fees or access to specialized &quot;cleaners&quot; for high-profile heist items? AI responses may include mistakes. Learn more "/>
</node>
</node>
